```java
void print(Object o) { System.out.println("Object: " + o); }
void print(String s) { System.out.println("String: " + s); }

Object value = "hello";
print(value); // prints "Object: hello", NOT "String: hello"
```

The surprise: even though `value` holds a `String` at runtime, overload resolution happens at **compile time**, using the *declared* type of `value` (`Object`). The compiler never looks at what's actually stored in the variable when picking which overload to call.

```java
class Shape {
    double area() { return 0; }
}
class Circle extends Shape {
    @Override
    double area() { return Math.PI * radius * radius; }
}

Shape s = new Circle();
s.area(); // runs Circle's area(), NOT Shape's
```

Unlike the overload example, this **does** dispatch on the runtime object — `s` is declared as `Shape`, but the JVM looks at what `s` actually points to (`Circle`) at the moment of the call and runs that version. This is the entire mechanism behind polymorphism.
